{"reference":"Ephesians 2","book":"Ephesians","chapter":2,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter moves from God’s gracious saving action to the creation of a reconciled community. Verses 8–10 hold together salvation by grace through faith and a life created for good works. Verses 11–18 then describe the Messiah as peace, making Jewish and gentile believers one; verses 19–22 extend that picture through citizenship, household, and a growing holy-temple metaphor.","verse_refs":["Ephesians 2:11-18","Ephesians 2:19-22","Ephesians 2:8-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ephesians_002_622dc19fdfdb1f9b5553f534"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Reading the Images Carefully","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"“Dead through trespasses” is theological rhetoric about the human condition in the passage, not a diagnosis of disability, depression, trauma, or intellectual incapacity. “Uncircumcision” reports a social label, not language that permits ethnic contempt or coercion. Likewise, the household and temple images portray a community; they do not turn the passage into a warrant for nationalism, territorial claims, forced conversion, or the power of sacred buildings.","verse_refs":["Ephesians 2:1-5","Ephesians 2:11","Ephesians 2:19-22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ephesians_002_0383d235962bdfce6058bfdb"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Questions Raised by Reconciliation","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Several expressions in verses 11–20 require care. The “dividing wall” may refer to temple exclusion, a Torah boundary, or hostility as a metaphor; the available evidence does not settle one referent. The scope of the abolition language in verse 15 is also disputed. These claims should not portray Jews, Judaism, Torah, or commandments as uniquely hostile or legalistic, nor should “one new humanity” be read as erasing Jewish and gentile histories or ethnicity. The reference to apostles and prophets as a foundation also has multiple readings and does not by itself establish authoritarian succession or clerical immunity.","verse_refs":["Ephesians 2:11-13","Ephesians 2:14","Ephesians 2:15","Ephesians 2:20"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ephesians_002_2a8de2567c8a2cd999bb5ab0"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_4","text":"The pronoun translated “this” in verse 8 does not, by gender and syntax alone, identify faith itself as the gift. The passage nevertheless explicitly joins grace through faith with good works. Its use of “flesh” in verse 3 should not be made a warrant for body shame, coercion, misogyny, or neglect of care.","verse_refs":["Ephesians 2:3","Ephesians 2:8","Ephesians 2:8-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ephesians_002_9450c24fc164d37acbdfa483"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_5","text":"The temple language in verses 19–22 describes the community as a dwelling for God in the Spirit. Sanctuary texts can share language of dwelling and holiness while retaining distinct referents: tabernacle, temples, Christological metaphor, community metaphor, heavenly sanctuary, and new creation should not simply be collapsed into one another. Later readings of this imagery cannot authorize antisemitism, replacement claims, seizure or destruction of holy places, or freedom from ethical accountability.","verse_refs":["Ephesians 2:11-22","Ephesians 2:19-22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ephesians_002_3461cff9c095c17caaa47275"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Grace and Good Works","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"The pairing of grace through faith with good works resists two opposite distortions: earned superiority on one side, and the claim that ethics, repair, labor justice, or care no longer matter on the other.","verse_refs":["Ephesians 2:3","Ephesians 2:8","Ephesians 2:8-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ephesians_002_4cc9a279c8b478182d0b6f82"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Within Ephesians","blocks":[{"id":"block_7","text":"Ephesians and Colossians share extensive language and structure. The direction and mechanism of any dependence between them remain debated.","verse_refs":["Ephesians 1:1-23","Ephesians 3:1-21","Ephesians 4:1-32","Ephesians 5:1-33","Ephesians 6:1-24"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ephesians_002_7f92732eaf5edf73ffa057ba"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}